HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (<i>italic</i> = abstract, never <code>new</code>-ed; '
 'dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. '
 'Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = extends or implements. Filled diamond = owns: each cache '
 'owns its order &mdash; one list for LRU, one list per use-count for LFU &mdash; and the list owns the nodes. Plain '
 'arrow = references. Dashed lavender = handed in through <code>configure()</code>. Dotted blue = notifies. '
 '<b>Where state lives:</b> a <code>Node</code> holds the key, the value, its two links, its use-count and its expiry, '
 'and it is the only object with per-entry state; <code>RecencyList</code> holds nothing but two sentinels and a count, '
 'and does nothing but splice; <code>LockedCache</code> holds the one lock and the three handed-in rules; each cache '
 'holds its capacity, its map and its order. Follow the two arrows into <code>Node</code>: the map points at it and the '
 'list points at it, and that is the entire design &mdash; two indexes over one object. Notice what is <i>not</i> here: '
 'no Entry class separate from the node (they are the same thing, which is what makes eviction O(1)), and no policy '
 'field on the cache, because the policy is the class.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does and what it guarantees; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the '
 'pointer surgery. Each copy button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every '
 'follow-up\'s reference code, with an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (nine claims, '
 'thirty-two checks; <code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints '
 'ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your five to eight clarifying questions (thread safety and what happens on a miss first); then type '
 'in the order of Main.java: the EvictionCause enum, Node, RecencyList with its two sentinels, the three one-method '
 'interfaces (Clock, CacheLoader, EvictionListener), the Cache interface, then LruCache with its lock and the order inside '
 'put, then a main that fills a cache of three, reads one entry and shows the right key leaving. Write the lock straight '
 'into LruCache in the hour; LockedCache is what you factor out on the second pass, or the moment the interviewer says '
 '"now do LFU".</div></div>')

FU = [
("The interviewer changes the policy mid-round: evict the least-FREQUENTLY used instead. How much of your code moves?", "twist", 10,
 "One new class, and nothing else. Callers hold a <code>Cache</code>, so the constructor line is the only edit at the "
 "call site. Inside, the single recency list becomes one recency list per use-count plus the smallest count currently "
 "in use: a get unlinks the node from its bucket, increments the count, links it at the front of the next bucket up, "
 "and if it just emptied the bottom bucket the smallest count moves up by one. An eviction takes the tail of the "
 "smallest bucket, so the tie between two entries used the same number of times is broken by recency &mdash; that "
 "tie-break is what the interviewer is checking for, and it is free because each bucket is itself a recency list. "
 "Everything stays O(1): no heap, no sort. The one honest wart is on the cold path, where an explicit remove can empty "
 "the smallest bucket without an insert following, and the smallest count is recomputed over the distinct use-counts "
 "rather than the entries.",
 sect(src, "final class LfuCache", "class WriteBackListener")),
("Fifty threads hammering one cache of a hundred. Prove nothing is lost and the count never goes over capacity, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading the count and writing the two indexes. Every operation does the whole read-modify-write "
 "&mdash; the lookup, the unlink of the victim from both the list and the map, and the link of the newcomer &mdash; "
 "inside one lock held by the cache, so no other writer can run in that gap. The proof is arithmetic rather than a "
 "count: fifty threads wait on one latch, then insert four hundred distinct keys each, and afterwards the number the "
 "listener reported as evicted plus the number still resident must be exactly twenty thousand. A lost update leaves it "
 "short; a double count leaves it over. The test also samples the size after every put and asserts it was never once "
 "above the capacity, and finally walks the list and compares its keys to the map's key set.",
 T("        // 5. fifty threads", "        // 6. an eviction listener")),
("One lock on a cache doing two hundred thousand gets a second. Have you serialised the whole system?", "non-functional", 5,
 "No, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part of a get is one hash lookup and six pointer writes: about eighty "
 "nanoseconds. Two hundred thousand of those is sixteen milliseconds of lock in every second, one and a half per cent "
 "busy, so a thread almost never finds it taken. Unlike most LLD problems a cache really can get hot, though: at five "
 "million operations a second the same lock is forty per cent busy and threads queue. The ladder, in order. Rung one is "
 "already built and is worth more than the rest put together: the loader (five milliseconds) and the write-back "
 "listener (two milliseconds) are outside the lock. Rung two is striping &mdash; sixteen segments by key hash, each "
 "with its own lock, its own map and its own order &mdash; and you must say the cost: there is no longer a global "
 "recency order, so it is approximate LRU, which is exactly what Guava ships. Rung three is Caffeine's: stop "
 "reordering on every read, buffer reads per thread and replay them in batches under a tryLock.",
 X("striping", "the stampede")),
("The database behind your loader is down. What is the state of the cache, and show me the code handling it.", "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was. A miss leaves the lock first and only then calls the loader, so an exception propagates to the "
 "caller with nothing written: no placeholder, no null entry, no half-linked node, and the next call retries cleanly. A "
 "loader that returns null is the same story &mdash; a miss stays a miss. That ordering is deliberate and it is the "
 "analogue of paying before committing: the slow, fallible, external step happens before any state change, so there is "
 "nothing to roll back. It also means a cold key hit by a thousand threads at once makes a thousand database calls, "
 "which is the honest cost of keeping the loader outside the lock; the fix is not to move it back in but to wrap the "
 "loader so duplicate work collapses, which is the next question but one.",
 sect(src, "    public V get(K key) {", "    public V put(K key, V value) {")),
("Why is this actually O(1)? Walk me through the eviction, pointer by pointer.", "non-functional", 5,
 "Three separate reasons, and an interviewer wants all three. First, the map answers \"the value for this key\" in one "
 "hash lookup. Second, the list is <i>doubly</i> linked, so unlinking a node found through the map is four pointer "
 "writes with no walk to find its predecessor; with a singly linked list it would be O(n) and the whole claim "
 "collapses. Third, the key is stored inside the node, so when eviction takes the tail it already has the key it needs "
 "to delete the map row &mdash; otherwise you would be searching the map by value. The two sentinels are the fourth, "
 "smaller reason: they remove every empty-list and one-element special case, so the splices are straight-line code and "
 "far harder to get wrong. Space is O(capacity): one map entry and one node per key, and a hit allocates nothing at "
 "all.",
 sect(src, "final class Node", "abstract class LockedCache")),
("A thousand threads miss the same cold key at the same instant. What happens to your database?", "non-functional", 5,
 "A thousand reads, unless you do something, because the loader deliberately runs outside the lock. The fix is not to "
 "put it back inside the lock &mdash; that would serialise every miss in the cache behind one slow key. It is to wrap "
 "the loader in another loader that collapses duplicate work: the first caller for a key installs a task in a "
 "concurrent map and runs it, everybody else finds the task already there and waits on its result, and the winner "
 "clears the slot when it is done. A thousand callers, one database read, and the same Decorator seam one level down "
 "&mdash; a <code>CacheLoader</code> wrapping a <code>CacheLoader</code>. The small race that remains is worth "
 "admitting: a caller arriving in the instant between the winner finishing and clearing the slot starts a second load.",
 X("the stampede", "the sweeper")),
("Entries have to expire sixty seconds after they are written. And how do you test that without sleeping?", "twist", 10,
 "Expiry is a second reason to evict, so it goes on the entry: each node carries the instant it stops being valid, "
 "stamped from an injected clock when it is written. The check is lazy &mdash; a read that finds an expired node "
 "treats it as a miss, unlinks it there and then, and tells the listener the cause was EXPIRED rather than CAPACITY. "
 "Lazy is enough for almost everything, and it costs one comparison on the hot path. What it does not do is free "
 "memory held by a key nobody ever asks for again, so the proactive version is a second index ordered by expiry &mdash; "
 "a heap here, a hashed timing wheel in a real system &mdash; that a background tick drains. The test does not sleep: "
 "it hands the cache a clock that returns a number a test controls, checks a hit one millisecond before the minute is "
 "up and a miss one millisecond after.",
 T("        // 7. TTL, on an injected clock", "        // 8. LFU evicts") + "\n" + X("the sweeper", "write-behind")),
("Bound it by memory instead of by entry count: values are between one kilobyte and one megabyte.", "twist", 5,
 "The capacity becomes a budget and every value declares a weight through a function handed in at construction. Two "
 "things change and both matter. The eviction becomes a <i>loop</i> rather than an if, because one large value can "
 "push out several small ones; and the new invariant &mdash; the sum of the weights never exceeds the budget &mdash; "
 "is checked and restored inside the same lock, so when the lock is released either the budget holds or nothing was "
 "written. A value larger than the whole budget is refused up front rather than emptying the cache to make room for "
 "something that still will not fit. Everything else is untouched: same node, same list, same listener, same tests.",
 X("bound by bytes", "striping")),
("Ops want the hit rate on a dashboard by Friday. Do not touch either cache class.", "functional", 5,
 "Another <code>Cache</code> that delegates and counts. Every method forwards to the one it wraps; get adds one to hits "
 "or misses on the way back, and hitRate is a division. It composes with anything, because the seam it plugs into is "
 "the interface rather than a class, so the same wrapper works over LRU, LFU, the weighted cache and the striped one. "
 "The honest caveat to say out loud: with a read-through loader a miss that the loader satisfies comes back non-null, "
 "so the wrapper records it as a hit &mdash; when that distinction matters, count the loader's calls instead, which is "
 "what the demo does.",
 X("stats", "the locking decorator")),
("Make it thread-safe by wrapping it, the way the textbook says. What does that cost you?", "design", 5,
 "It is correct, it is five lines, and it is the right move when the class you were handed is not yours to change. "
 "What it costs is the one thing this design cares about: everything the inner cache does now happens inside the "
 "wrapper's lock, including the read-through loader against a database and the write-back listener against a store. "
 "Only the owner of a lock can release it and <i>then</i> call the listener, so from the outside there is no way to "
 "keep the slow things out of the critical section &mdash; which is why the caches in Main.java hold their own lock. "
 "Worth adding: a read-write lock buys nothing here either, because a get moves a node to the front, so there are no "
 "pure readers to let through in parallel.",
 X("the locking decorator", "bound by bytes")),
("Now it has to survive a restart, and there are two servers.", "twist", 5,
 "Memory goes in front of a shared store, and the seams are already there. The store becomes the loader, so a local "
 "miss falls through to Redis or a table without the cache knowing what it is talking to. A write goes to the store "
 "first and only then to the local copy, so a store that refuses leaves this server holding the old value rather than "
 "a lie. The store's write is a conditional update by version &mdash; only if this version is newer than what is there "
 "&mdash; which is the database doing exactly what the lock does in one process: one atomic step decides the winner "
 "and the loser simply loses. What is left is the hard part of every distributed cache: the other server's copy is now "
 "wrong, so a write publishes an invalidation message and each server drops that key. Say out loud that this is "
 "eventually consistent, and that the window is the message's delivery time.",
 X("two tiers", "the builder")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you test what an entry was stamped with?", "design", 3,
 "The cache has a <code>Clock</code> it was handed, and it is the only thing in the system that reads the time: the "
 "expiry stamp on a node comes from it, and so does the expiry check. A test hands in a clock that returns a number the "
 "test controls, writes an entry, moves the number past the TTL and asserts the miss &mdash; nothing sleeps, and the "
 "boundary can be probed to the millisecond on either side. The same seam makes the sweeper testable: the sweep is "
 "given a now, not asked to find one.",
 "/** Where time comes from. Injected, so a TTL test can jump an hour forward without sleeping for an hour. */\n"
 "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }\n\n"
 "// on the cache: handed in, defaulted, never read from the wall clock inside a method\n"
 "private Clock clock = System::currentTimeMillis;\n"
 "void setClock(Clock c) { this.clock = Objects.requireNonNull(c); }\n"
 "long stamp()             { return ttlMs <= 0 ? 0 : clock.nowMs() + ttlMs; }\n"
 "boolean expired(Node<K, V> n) { return n.expireAtMs > 0 && clock.nowMs() >= n.expireAtMs; }\n\n"
 "// in a test: pick the instant, then move it across the boundary\n"
 "long[] now = { 1_700_000_000_000L };\n"
 "ttl.setClock(() -> now[0]);\n"
 "ttl.setTtlMs(60_000);\n"
 "ttl.put(\"session\", \"abc\");\n"
 "now[0] += 59_999;   // still a hit\n"
 "now[0] += 2;        // now a miss, and the entry is unlinked on that read\n"),
("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and where would a Factory or a Builder earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "None of the patterns was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3, and it is unusually "
 "clean here because the policy is the whole implementation rather than a field. Decorator is the same move's wrapper, "
 "and this problem has three of them &mdash; stats, locking, and a loader that wraps a loader to collapse a stampede. "
 "Observer is move 4's rule that a slow listener must not be inside the lock. State is move 6, the entry's life and "
 "the cause on the callback. Then the one that differs from every other problem in this set: Builder <i>earns</i> its "
 "place here. Capacity, TTL, policy, loader, listener, clock and stats are seven knobs of which six are optional, "
 "which is exactly why Guava ships CacheBuilder and Caffeine ships Caffeine.newBuilder; the rule has not changed, the "
 "optional fields have. Factory is one ternary inside build() and grows a name the day policies arrive as strings "
 "from config. Template Method was declined: LockedCache holds the lock and three helpers but does not own get or "
 "put, because reading either cache on its own is worth more than the lines it would save. Singleton earned nothing. "
 "For SOLID: S is move 2 (Node holds links, RecencyList splices, the cache owns the policy and the lock); O is LFU "
 "and StatsCache, neither of which opened LruCache; L is that the caller never asks which policy it got, with the "
 "trap that a decorator forgetting to delegate a method breaks substitution while still compiling; I is three "
 "one-method interfaces; D is configure() and setClock(), which is why a test can hand in a loader that throws.",
 X("the builder", "the five-line version")),
("Should I have just used LinkedHashMap?", "design", 5,
 "Often, yes &mdash; and saying so is a senior signal, not a cop-out. A <code>LinkedHashMap</code> built with access "
 "order is exactly this design: a hash map whose entries are already in a doubly linked list, with "
 "<code>removeEldestEntry</code> as the eviction hook. Five lines, in the JDK since 1.4, and the right answer for a "
 "single-threaded cache with no TTL and no callback. Three things push you back to writing the nodes out, and they are "
 "the three the interviewer will ask for within ten minutes: you cannot see the victim as it goes, only vote on "
 "whether it should (the hook returns a boolean); there is no per-entry expiry; and it is not thread-safe, so it needs "
 "a wrapper, which drags the loader into the lock. So: name it, say when it is enough, and say which of those three "
 "made you hand-roll.",
 X("the five-line version", "Runs every extension")),
]

